<TeXmacs|2.1.5>

<style|article>

<\body>
  <section|Words>

  A <em|ligature><index|ligature> joins two letters into one glyph, and
  <em|kerning><index|kerning> moves a pair of letters closer together or
  further apart. A <em|glyph><index|glyph><glossary-explain|glyph|the shape
  which a font draws for a character> is drawn by a font<index|font>.
  Fonts come in families<subindex|font|family> and
  shapes<subindex|font|shape>, such as the italic
  shape<subsubindex|font|shape|italic>.

  <new-page>

  <section|Lines>

  Paragraphs are broken into lines<index|line breaking> and lines into
  pages<index|page breaking>. Kerning<index|kerning> also applies
  across a line, and every font<index|font> has a design
  size<subindex|font|design size>. The
  <em|measure><glossary-explain|measure|the width of a line of text> is the
  width of the column, and the <em|leading><glossary-explain|leading|the
  distance between the baselines of two lines> its vertical spacing.

  <new-page>

  <section|Pages>

  A page<index|page breaking> holds a number of lines, and its
  footnotes<index|footnote>. A <em|widow><glossary|widow> is the last line
  of a paragraph left alone at the top of a page.

  <\the-glossary|gly>
  </the-glossary>

  <\the-index|idx>
  </the-index>
</body>

